# APML Interface: In-app browser overlay
# SSI Learning App
# Version: 1.0.0
# Last updated: 2026-08-19 (A-178: the Why-this-works proof rows now open
#   named saysomethingin.com intensive pages through this overlay)
# Created: 2026-08-18 (owner ruling of 2026-08-18 that
#               saysomethingin.com links should open inside the app)
# Source: packages/player-vue/src/composables/useInAppBrowser.ts
#         packages/player-vue/src/components/InAppBrowser.vue
#         packages/player-vue/src/App.vue (global mount)

## ============================================================================
## OVERVIEW
## ============================================================================

SPECIFICATION:
  name: "In-app browser"
  purpose: |
    Open saysomethingin.com pages in a full-screen sheet OVER the app, so a
    learner following a link does not leave the installed PWA and does not
    lose their place in a session.
  origin: |
    Owner ruling, 2026-08-18, after playing the dev build:
      "we can possibly send people to our website, which is saysomethingin.com,
       and that should probably open within the app, rather than go outside of
       things. Probably should be a browser that opens within the app."
  principle: |
    This app is a PWA — there is no Capacitor, Cordova or native shell, so
    there is no system in-app-browser to call. "A browser inside the app" is
    therefore a Vue overlay wrapping an iframe, and it is bounded by what the
    remote host permits.

    The learner is NEVER shown a silent blank sheet. That is the single
    failure mode this design exists to prevent.

## ============================================================================
## THE FRAMING CONSTRAINT
## ============================================================================

CONSTRAINT:
  problem: |
    A page can forbid being iframed with X-Frame-Options or a frame-ancestors
    CSP. The browser gives JavaScript NO way to read those headers
    cross-origin, and no reliable way to observe the refusal — a blocked
    iframe still fires `load` in Chromium.
  decision: "Host allowlist, not a runtime probe."
  rationale: |
    Frameability is a fixed property of a host, not of a tap. A probe would
    cost a server round-trip on every link to learn something already known.
    The allowlist is deterministic, free at runtime, and honest about the
    limit of its knowledge.
  verified_headers:
    # Checked live 2026-08-18. If these change, change the allowlist.
    - host: "www.saysomethingin.com"       result: "200, no XFO, no frame-ancestors — FRAMES"
    - host: "saysomethingin.com"           result: "same site — FRAMES"
    - host: "en.saysomethingin.com"        result: "X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN — REFUSES"
    - host: "en.forum.saysomethingin.com"  result: "X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN — REFUSES"
  https_only: |
    Only https URLs are framed. An http page inside an https app is blocked as
    mixed content, and a non-http(s) scheme is not ours to frame at all.

## ============================================================================
## BEHAVIOUR
## ============================================================================

FLOW:
  entry: "openInApp(url, title?) — the only call site API."

  frameable_host:
    - "Overlay opens; page renders in the iframe; app state is untouched."
    - "Returns 'in-app'."

  known_refusing_host:
    - "No overlay at all — hands straight off to a real browser tab."
    - "Returns 'external'."
    - rationale: |
        Showing a card that says 'this opens in your browser' BEFORE doing the
        thing the learner already asked for is a tax, not a courtesy. This path
        is no worse than the target=_blank behaviour it replaces.

  frameable_host_that_fails_to_load:
    - "After FRAME_LOAD_TIMEOUT_MS (8000) with no `load`, the sheet shows an
       honest card: 'This one opens in your browser.' plus a tap-through."
    - "The tap-through closes the sheet and opens a real browser tab."
    - rationale: "This is the unexpected case; a white sheet is what we refuse."

  exits:
    - "Close button in the overlay header."
    - "Escape key — a guaranteed exit that does not depend on the page loading."

## ============================================================================
## CHROME AND THEME
## ============================================================================

CHROME:
  safe_areas: |
    The sheet is fixed, edge-anchored chrome, so per CLAUDE.md the header
    grows by env(safe-area-inset-top) AND pads its content down by the same
    amount — with border-box sizing the inset must be EXTRA height, not eaten
    height, or the close button lands under the iOS status bar. Horizontal
    padding uses max(base, env(safe-area-inset-left/right)). The body pads by
    env(safe-area-inset-bottom) to clear the home indicator.
  theme: |
    Mist only — warm-grey canvas, white elevated header, one belt-coloured
    accent on the fallback action. No dark mode, no theme switcher.
  header_shows: "The given title, with the host beneath it."

## ============================================================================
## CALL SITES
## ============================================================================

CALL_SITES:
  converted:
    - "components/SettingsScreen.vue — forum link (refuses framing → external)"
    - "components/SettingsScreen.vue — classic Welsh listening (refuses → external)"
    - "containers/SchoolsContainer.vue — 'Back to saysomethingin.com' (frames)"
    - "views/TryLinkGateway.vue — 'Visit SaySomethingin' (frames)"
    - "components/me/WhyThisWorks.vue — the five proof rows on the
       'Where all this comes from' block (all frame). These are buttons, not
       converted anchors: the rows are data in explainer/learnerExplainers.ts
       (ExplainerBlock.links), so openInApp is the only path and there is no
       href to fall back to."
  note: |
    Converted anchors keep their href plus target=_blank/rel=noopener as a
    no-JS fallback; the @click.prevent handler is what routes them.

  deliberately_untouched:
    - what: "mailto: links (RedeemCode.vue, SignInModal.vue, Onboarding.vue)"
      why:  "Must keep handing off to the learner's mail client."
    - what: "Paddle checkout/portal window.open (composables/useSubscription.ts)"
      why:  "A payment flow. Framing a payment provider is a bad idea and
             Paddle's own overlay already owns that surface."
    - what: "Learner-story URLs in Onboarding.vue"
      why:  "Present only as source comments, not as live links."
    - what: "The RTÉ Raidió na Gaeltachta clip receipting the Irish proof row
             (rte.ie/radio/rnag/clips/22557598)"
      why:  "Verified live 2026-08-19: rte.ie sends X-Frame-Options SAMEORIGIN,
             so it cannot be framed, and linking it would throw a standalone-PWA
             learner out to Safari mid-session. The broadcast is named in the
             row's copy instead and the URL kept as a source comment in
             explainer/learnerExplainers.ts — the row itself goes to our own
             /intensive-ireland page, which carries the interview."

## ============================================================================
## TESTS
## ============================================================================

TESTS:
  file: "packages/player-vue/src/composables/useInAppBrowser.test.ts"
  covers:
    - "Allowlist: frames / refuses / unknown host / non-https / unparseable"
    - "openInApp returns 'in-app' vs 'external' and never opens an empty sheet"
    - "Overlay renders nothing until opened; close button and Escape both exit"
    - "Load-timeout raises the fallback; a timely load does not"
    - "Fallback tap-through opens the real browser and closes the sheet"
